@* Using the library (UI docs): the view imports, the three assets in order, the icon sprite and a first screen. *@

<p>DotNetForge UI is a Razor class library (<code>src/DotNetForge.UI</code>). Using it takes three things: the tag
    helpers registered in your views, three static files loaded by your layout, and the icon sprite, which the library
    finds by itself. An admin screen already has all three - the admin layout and the admin view imports provide them.
    A layout of your own (or an <a href="@UiDocsCatalog.Root/extensions">extension's</a>) adds them as shown below.</p>

<h2 id="admin-screens">In an admin screen</h2>
<p>Nothing to set up. <code>Areas/Admin/Views/_ViewImports.cshtml</code> registers the tag helpers, and the admin layout
    (<code>_AdminLayout.cshtml</code>) loads the theme script, the stylesheet and <code>dnf.js</code>, renders the toast
    region and the confirmation dialog, and draws the screen's title. A screen sets <code>ViewData["Title"]</code>
    (the page's only <code>h1</code>), optionally <code>ViewData["Description"]</code>, puts its header buttons in
    <code>@@section Actions</code> and writes its content with the components - see <a href="#first-screen">a complete
    screen</a>.</p>

<h2 id="view-imports">View imports</h2>
<p>Add these lines to the <code>_ViewImports.cshtml</code> of the view folder (both <code>_ViewImports.cshtml</code>
    files of the web host have them):</p>

<ui-doc-code id="imports" lang="razor">
@@addTagHelper *, DotNetForge.UI
@@using DotNetForge.UI
@@using DotNetForge.UI.Components
</ui-doc-code>

<table>
    <thead><tr><th>Line</th><th>Gives you</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>@@addTagHelper *, DotNetForge.UI</code></td><td>the <code>&lt;dnf-*&gt;</code> tag helpers: <code>&lt;dnf-button&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;dnf-icon&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;dnf-badge&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;dnf-dropdown&gt;</code> …</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>@@using DotNetForge.UI</code></td><td>the enums <code>DnfSize</code>, <code>DnfTone</code>, <code>DnfButtonVariant</code> and <code>DnfAssets</code> (the asset paths)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>@@using DotNetForge.UI.Components</code></td><td>the tag helper classes, for their constants (e.g. <code>DnfTimestampTagHelper.Relative</code>)</td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>
<p>Every component also works without a tag helper: the classes are the API, so a view without these lines can still
    write <code>&lt;button class="dnf-button dnf-button--primary"&gt;</code>.</p>

<h2 id="assets">Assets</h2>
<p>The library ships three files, served as static web assets under <code>/_content/DotNetForge.UI/</code>. A layout of
    your own loads them in this order:</p>

<ui-doc-code id="assets-head" lang="razor">
<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <meta name="color-scheme" content="light dark" />
    @* 1. synchronously, before the stylesheet: the saved theme applies before the first paint *@
    <script src="~/_content/DotNetForge.UI/dnf-theme.js" asp-append-version="true"></script>
    @* 2. tokens, base styles, components and utilities in one file *@
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="~/_content/DotNetForge.UI/dnf.css" asp-append-version="true" />
    @* your own stylesheets after it *@
    @* 3. behaviours, deferred *@
    <script src="~/_content/DotNetForge.UI/dnf.js" asp-append-version="true" defer></script>
</head>
</ui-doc-code>

<ol>
    <li><strong><code>dnf-theme.js</code></strong> - in <code>&lt;head&gt;</code>, without <code>defer</code>. It sets
        <code>&lt;html data-theme&gt;</code> from the user's saved choice before anything is drawn (no flash of the wrong
        theme) and marks <code>&lt;html class="dnf-js"&gt;</code>, so <code>dnf-js-only</code> controls show from the first
        paint (<a href="@UiDocsCatalog.Root/theming#switching">Theming</a>).</li>
    <li><strong><code>dnf.css</code></strong> - the tokens, the base styles, every component and the Tailwind utilities,
        compiled at build time into one file. Link your own stylesheets after it.</li>
    <li><strong><code>dnf.js</code></strong> - with <code>defer</code>. The <code>data-*</code> hooks and
        <code>window.dnfUI</code> (<a href="@UiDocsCatalog.Root/behaviours">Behaviours</a>). Optional: a page that uses no
        hook can leave it out.</li>
</ol>

<table>
    <thead><tr><th><code>DnfAssets</code> constant</th><th>Path</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>DnfAssets.ThemeScript</code></td><td><code>/_content/DotNetForge.UI/dnf-theme.js</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>DnfAssets.Stylesheet</code></td><td><code>/_content/DotNetForge.UI/dnf.css</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>DnfAssets.Script</code></td><td><code>/_content/DotNetForge.UI/dnf.js</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>DnfAssets.Sprite</code></td><td><code>/_content/DotNetForge.UI/icons.svg</code></td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>
<p><code>asp-append-version</code> (a cache-busting <code>?v=</code>) needs <code>@@addTagHelper *, Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.TagHelpers</code>;
    without it, write the plain paths. The constants hold the same paths and include no app path base.</p>
<p>Every view and script is Content-Security-Policy safe: no inline <code>&lt;script&gt;</code>, no <code>&lt;style&gt;</code>
    block, no <code>style</code> or <code>on…</code> attribute. Keep your own markup that way - behaviour goes into
    <code>data-*</code> hooks and script files, looks into stylesheets.</p>

<h2 id="sprite">The icon sprite</h2>
<p>Icons are one SVG sprite, <code>icons.svg</code>, with a <code>&lt;symbol id&gt;</code> per icon. Nothing is inlined into
    the page: <code>&lt;dnf-icon&gt;</code> writes an <code>&lt;svg&gt;</code> that references the symbol by the
    cache-busted sprite URL, and <code>dnf.js</code> reuses the first sprite URL it finds on the page for the icons it
    creates (<code>dnfUI.icon</code>). Icons are drawn with strokes in the current text colour. Every icon id:
    <a href="@UiDocsCatalog.Root/components/icon">Icon</a>.</p>

<ui-doc-example id="sprite-icons" title="Icons from the sprite" description="The tag helper and the plain markup it renders. Decorative icons are hidden from screen readers; give one a label only when it is the only thing that names something.">
    <dnf-icon name="pages" />
    <dnf-icon name="image" size="Lg" />
    <dnf-icon name="trash" label="Deleted" class="text-danger" />
    <svg class="dnf-icon" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><use href="/_content/DotNetForge.UI/icons.svg#webhook"></use></svg>
</ui-doc-example>

<h2 id="feedback">Toasts and the confirmation dialog</h2>
<p><code>dnfUI.toast</code> and <code>data-confirm</code> need markup in the layout: a toast region with its template, and
    the confirmation dialog. CMS layouts render <code>&lt;partial name="_Feedback" /&gt;</code>
    (<code>Views/Shared/_Feedback.cshtml</code>), which also turns <code>TempData["Success"]</code>,
    <code>["Error"]</code> and <code>["Info"]</code> into toasts. Elsewhere, copy the markup from
    <a href="@UiDocsCatalog.Root/behaviours#toasts">Behaviours</a>; without the dialog, <code>data-confirm</code> falls
    back to the browser's <code>confirm()</code>.</p>

<h2 id="custom-layout">A layout of your own</h2>
<p>A complete document that uses the library - the shape of the sign-in layout and of an extension's admin tab:</p>

<ui-doc-code id="layout" lang="razor">
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <meta name="color-scheme" content="light dark" />
    <title>@@ViewData["Title"]</title>
    <script src="~/_content/DotNetForge.UI/dnf-theme.js" asp-append-version="true"></script>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="~/_content/DotNetForge.UI/dnf.css" asp-append-version="true" />
    <script src="~/_content/DotNetForge.UI/dnf.js" asp-append-version="true" defer></script>
</head>
<body>
    <a class="dnf-skip-link" href="#main">Skip to content</a>
    <main id="main" class="dnf-container" tabindex="-1">
        @@RenderBody()
    </main>
    <partial name="_Feedback" />
</body>
</html>
</ui-doc-code>

<h2 id="first-screen">A complete screen</h2>
<p>An admin screen is a view in <code>Areas/Admin/Views/</code> rendered in the admin layout: two lines of
    <code>ViewData</code>, the header actions, and the content as components.</p>

<ui-doc-code id="screen-view" lang="razor">
@@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Newsletters";
    ViewData["Description"] = "Emails sent to everyone who subscribed on the site.";
}

@@section Actions {
    <dnf-button variant="Primary" icon="plus" href="/admin/newsletters/new">New newsletter</dnf-button>
}

<section class="dnf-card" aria-labelledby="newsletters-title">
    <div class="dnf-card__header">
        <h2 class="dnf-card__title" id="newsletters-title">Newsletters</h2>
        <dnf-badge>3</dnf-badge>
    </div>
    <div class="dnf-table dnf-table--stack">
        <table class="dnf-table__table">…</table>
    </div>
</section>
</ui-doc-code>

<p>The content, live:</p>

<ui-doc-example id="screen-preview" title="The screen's content" description="A card with a table that stacks on phones, status badges and row actions." layout="block">
